---
import PublicContentLayout from '../../layouts/PublicContentLayout.astro';

export const prerender = true;

const title = 'Grok Build as a T3 Verified Resume Agent | Reinstate';
const description =
  'Grok Build is T3: Reinstate reads local sessions, builds a portable capsule, and launches grok --resume against the vendor session. Encrypted sync is not supported.';
---

<PublicContentLayout
  {title}
  {description}
  path="/integrations/grok"
  eyebrow="Integration · Grok Build · T3"
  answer="Grok Build is T3: a read-only local index, a handoff source, and same-vendor native resume through the vendor's own CLI. Reinstate is not a handoff destination for Grok and does not sync Grok sessions."
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Integrations', path: '/integrations' },
    { name: 'Grok Build', path: '/integrations/grok' },
  ]}
  tags={['Grok Build', 'T3 verified resume', 'handoff source']}
>
  <h2 id="support-at-a-glance">Grok Build support at a glance</h2>
  <dl class="pc-facts">
    <div>
      <dt>Tier</dt>
      <dd>T3 Verified resume</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Storage family</dt>
      <dd>F1 home tree</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Verified version range</dt>
      <dd>1.0.5 – 1.0.5</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Encrypted sync (T5)</dt>
      <dd>Not supported</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
  <p>
    T3 means <code>rein resume grok:&lt;id&gt;</code> launches
    <code>grok --resume &lt;uuid&gt;</code> against the vendor's own session, and
    <code>rein handoff --from grok</code> can build a portable capsule. Grok Build is not a
    T4 handoff destination and not a T5 sync agent.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-t3-includes">What T3 includes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>T1 read-only discovery, search, and inspect of local Grok Build sessions.</li>
    <li>T2 structured handoff from Grok Build into a new Claude Code or Codex session.</li>
    <li>
      T3 same-vendor <code>rein resume</code> and <code>rein fork</code>, the latter through
      <code>grok --resume &lt;uuid&gt; --fork-session</code>.
    </li>
    <li>A bounded <code>grok --version</code> probe with a fail-closed range of 1.0.5 – 1.0.5.</li>
    <li>Fail-closed behavior on an unrecognized layout or version (exit code <code>5</code>).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-t3-excludes">What T3 excludes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No <code>rein handoff --to grok</code> destination.</li>
    <li>No <code>rein push</code> / <code>rein pull</code> of Grok sessions.</li>
    <li>No write into Grok's session store.</li>
    <li>
      No resume of a session whose recorded id is not a UUID. <code>grok --resume</code> also
      accepts a session <em>title</em>, and titles are neither unique nor stable, so such a
      session stays read-only rather than being addressed by name.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="pc-callout">
    <p>Native resume is same-vendor: it launches Grok's own CLI against Grok's own session.</p>
    <p>
      A handoff starts a <em>new</em> Claude Code or Codex session. It does not reconstruct
      Grok history or translate the vendor transcript.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="evidence">Evidence</h2>
  <p>
    Committed fixtures live under <code>testdata/sessionindex/grok</code> and
    <code>testdata/handoff/grok</code>. There is no T3 version probe and no T3 device
    report. See the
    <a href="https://github.com/HarjjotSinghh/reinstate/blob/main/docs/session-storage/grok.md"
      >Grok storage page</a
    >
    and the <a href="/compatibility">tier matrix</a>.
  </p>

  <section class="pc-related" aria-labelledby="related-heading">
    <h2 id="related-heading">Related</h2>
    <nav aria-label="Related pages">
      <a href="/integrations">All integrations</a>
      <a href="/compatibility">Compatibility matrix</a>
      <a href="/docs/handoff">Structured handoff</a>
      <a href="/docs/adapters">Adapter documentation</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
</PublicContentLayout>
